1
00:00:00,919 --> 00:00:02,496
(Q31（notes）) Good afternoon, everyone!

2
00:00:02,496 --> 00:00:08,392
This is the first seminar in preparation for our archaeological fieldwork in Namibia;

3
00:00:08,392 --> 00:00:19,596
we are fantastically lucky to have received partial research funding for this trip from our Institute, so I shall expect 200% attention and participation from you all.

4
00:00:19,596 --> 00:00:35,532
[Q31答案句： 竟然是日期， 紧跟定位词啊 filedwork trip] First in this seminar, I'm going to give a brief introduction to contemporary research on rock art, and in the second part I'm going to give you some do's and don'ts for our fieldwork trip in April-so please listen very carefully.

5
00:00:35,532 --> 00:00:40,345
(Q32) I'm first going to focus on the interpretation of rock art in Namibia.

6
00:00:40,345 --> 00:00:46,904
We are very fortunate to be going to an area where you can find some of the most important sites in the entire world.

7
00:00:46,904 --> 00:00:56,776
And I hope to show you how easy it is for everyone to make mistakes in looking at cultures which are different from our own-the first and most important lesson we have to learn.

8
00:00:56,776 --> 00:01:03,482
In Namibia there are both paintings and engravings-that's where the surface of the rock is cut out.

9
00:01:03,482 --> 00:01:11,839
Many of the engravings show footprints of animals and most scholars used to think that the purpose of these was simple and obvious:

10
00:01:11,839 --> 00:01:21,612
[Q32答案句] this rock art was like a school book with pictures to teach children about tracks: which track belonged to which animal-giraffe, lion and so on.

11
00:01:21,612 --> 00:01:23,718
(Q33) But there were some mysteries.

12
00:01:23,718 --> 00:01:32,854
[Q33答案句] First, when you look at a typical Namibian painting or engraving, you see the tracks are repeated, there are dozens of tracks for the same animal.

13
00:01:32,854 --> 00:01:39,558
You'd expect just one clear illustration if the reason-the aim-was to teach tracking.

14
00:01:39,558 --> 00:01:42,558
(Q34) Now there were two more problems.

15
00:01:42,558 --> 00:01:52,011
Why are some of the engravings of animals very accurate as you'd expect-all clearly identifiable-and others quite unrealistic?

16
00:01:52,011 --> 00:02:00,203
[Q34答案句] And another mystery some of these unrealistic animals that's in the engravings- seem to be half human.

17
00:02:00,203 --> 00:02:03,256
Some, for example, have got human faces.

18
00:02:03,256 --> 00:02:08,985
(Q35) Many researchers now think that these were pictures the wise men engraved of themselves.

19
00:02:08,985 --> 00:02:16,157
[Q35答案句：答案前置 后置定语 n +prep.+ n,] They believed they could use magic to control the animals they had drawn, so the hunters could then catch them for food.

20
00:02:16,157 --> 00:02:24,023
This shows you some of the dangers of coming from one culture to another, as we'll be doing, without understanding it fully.

21
00:02:24,023 --> 00:02:37,262
Scholars imagined that children looked at rock art pictures to learn to track-just because they themselves had learnt skills from pictures; many researchers now believe that rock art had a much more complex purpose.

22
00:02:37,262 --> 00:02:39,944
And we'll talk more about it next week!

23
00:02:39,944 --> 00:02:51,750
(Q36(sentence)) Now before I invite you to join in a discussion in this second part of the seminar, I'd like to make some very important points about our fieldwork-and in fact any field trip to look at rock art.

24
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:55,824
We're going to a number of sites, and we won't always be together.

25
00:02:55,824 --> 00:03:06,731
The single largest problem faced by people who manage the sites is-yes, I'm sure you've guessed-damage caused by visitors, even though it's usually unintentional.

26
00:03:06,731 --> 00:03:15,345
Whenever you do go to a site, don't forget you can learn many things from observing at a distance instead of walking all over it.

27
00:03:15,345 --> 00:03:18,569
This can really help to reduce visitor pressure.

28
00:03:18,569 --> 00:03:27,520
People often say, 'Well, there's only two of us and just this one time', but maybe thousands of people are saying the same thing.

29
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:37,579
And then some basic rules to guide you-we'll have our own camp near a village, but remember never to camp on a site if you go on your own.

30
00:03:37,579 --> 00:03:45,692
It may be disrespectful to the people of that culture, and certainly don't make fires, however romantic it may seem.

31
00:03:45,692 --> 00:03:52,492
It's really dangerous in dry areas, and you can easily burn priceless undiscovered material by doing so.

32
00:03:52,492 --> 00:03:56,900
[观察与记录须知] So, how are we going to enjoy the rock art on our field trip?

33
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:04,016
By looking at it, drawing it and photographing it-NEVER by touching it or even tracing it.

34
00:04:04,016 --> 00:04:07,699
Rock art is fragile and precious.

35
00:04:07,699 --> 00:04:14,343
Remember that climbing on rocks and in caves can destroy in a moment what has lasted for centuries.

36
00:04:14,343 --> 00:04:17,482
So no heroics in Namibia, please!

37
00:04:17,482 --> 00:04:20,647
Try to be extra careful and help others to be too.

38
00:04:20,647 --> 00:04:31,531
And lastly please don't even move rocks or branches to take photographs - you should leave the site intact -I'm sure I can rely on you to do that.

39
00:04:31,531 --> 00:04:45,678
Well, that's about all I want to say before today's first discussion, but if you have any questions please ask them now and don't forget you'll find some fascinating information about world-wide sites on the Internet.

40
00:04:45,678 --> 00:04:48,178
Right, first question then?

